The converging pattern — palindrome check, executed:

```go
func isPalindrome(s string) bool {
    for i, j := 0, len(s)-1; i < j; i, j = i+1, j-1 {
        if s[i] != s[j] { return false }
    }
    return true
}
// isPalindrome("racecar") → true — verified
// isPalindrome("hello") → false — verified
```

The pattern: two pointers starting at both ends, moving toward each other. Each iteration compares, then advances. The loop ends when they cross. O(n) time, O(1) space.

When two-pointer wins: the data is ORDERED (sorted, or symmetric like a palindrome) and the answer is a pair or a position — the ordering lets you decide which pointer to move from the comparison, skipping n-1 pairs per step.

The three variants:
1. Converging (i from start, j from end) — palindrome, sorted two-sum (if the array is sorted: too small → i++, too big → j--)
2. Fast/slow (same direction, different speeds) — cycle detection (they meet), middle-finding (fast ends, slow is mid)
3. Dual-array (one pointer per array) — sorted merge, intersection

The trade vs. the hash map: two-sum on an UNSORTED array with no ordering — hash map O(n) time, O(n) space. Two-sum on a SORTED array — two-pointer O(n) time, O(1) space. The sortedness buys the space saving.
